WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.889
A ninety-nine million parameter model, first under two hundred and fifty million

00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:08.588
on a leaderboard, and fifty-six percent on an arithmetic test.

00:00:08.968 --> 00:00:12.419
The claim is that one change did it: a longer learning-rate decay.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:15.931
Hi, I'm Odile.

00:00:15.931 --> 00:00:18.308
I read this model's files and its benchmark.

00:00:18.308 --> 00:00:19.496
Here is what's inside.

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:25.150
Twenty-four layers, five hundred and twelve wide.

00:00:25.642 --> 00:00:28.475
Every fourth is attention, which caches every token.

00:00:28.475 --> 00:00:31.662
The other eighteen are recurrent, with a fixed memory.

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:35.464
Here is that memory, as a toy head.

00:00:35.464 --> 00:00:39.134
A token writes a value at a key, and reading that key returns it.

00:00:39.554 --> 00:00:41.342
A second key overlaps the first.

00:00:41.342 --> 00:00:43.429
Its write leaks through the shared channel.

00:00:44.154 --> 00:00:47.361
Rewrite the first key with one gate for erase and write.

00:00:47.361 --> 00:00:51.442
The new value lands, but the second key drops to zero point eight seven.

00:00:51.862 --> 00:00:54.949
Gated DeltaNet-2 gives erase and write their own gates.

00:00:54.949 --> 00:00:57.008
Erase everything, write at half strength.

00:00:57.008 --> 00:00:58.723
One gate cannot do that.

00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:03.110
KLM's layer follows the paper's block closely.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.673
Each gate has its own projection and its own sigmoid.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.808
Half the parameters are feed-forward.

00:01:09.808 --> 00:01:13.860
The embedding is two percent: the vocabulary is tiny, and numbers

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.333
are split into digits.

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:18.132
Now the decay.

00:01:18.132 --> 00:01:23.194
KLM 1.5 took its learning rate to zero over twenty billion tokens.

00:01:23.194 --> 00:01:25.725
KLM 1.6 takes a hundred billion.

00:01:26.105 --> 00:01:30.356
That also means eighty billion more tokens, a bigger batch, and new math data.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:31.571
Not just the schedule.

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:37.545
The arithmetic test offers four consecutive whole numbers for every question.

00:01:37.965 --> 00:01:40.185
In most, only the last digit differs.

00:01:40.185 --> 00:01:42.722
So the test mostly asks: which last digit?

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:48.041
On that test, two models under two hundred and fifty million score

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:50.184
higher, one of them smaller than KLM.

00:01:50.564 --> 00:01:54.003
KLM 1.6 is third under two hundred and fifty million.

00:01:54.003 --> 00:01:57.786
Its first place on the index is less than one point.

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:01.978
A careful small base model, with an honest card.

00:02:01.978 --> 00:02:05.593
But the decay was not the only change, and the lead rests

00:02:05.593 --> 00:02:06.798
on a last-digit test.

00:02:08.692 --> 00:02:12.211
Recurrent layers with separate gates, a longer decay with more data,

00:02:12.211 --> 00:02:13.490
and a narrow test.

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:14.450
Thanks for watching!

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.936
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:17.526
I'm Odile.

00:02:17.526 --> 00:02:17.821
Bye!
